# Java/Kotlin + Spring testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to JUnit 5 +
Spring Boot Test conventions, for both Java and Kotlin. Applies only to
`*.java`/`*.kt`/`*.kts` files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live under `src/test/java` or `src/test/kotlin`, mirroring the
  package of the class under test, named `<Type>Test` (unit) or
  `<Type>IT`/`<Type>IntegrationTest` (integration) — match whichever
  convention the project already uses.
- Prefer a plain, dependency-free unit test (construct the class
  directly, pass mocks/fakes to the constructor) for logic that does not
  need the Spring container; reserve `@SpringBootTest` for genuine
  integration coverage — a full context load per test class is slow and
  is not a substitute for testing a service's logic in isolation.

## Test slices

- Use the narrowest Spring test slice that covers what is being tested:
  `@WebMvcTest` for a controller (with `MockMvc`), `@DataJpaTest` for a
  repository, `@SpringBootTest` only when the interaction across the
  whole wired context is actually what is under test.
- For a repository/integration test that needs a real database, prefer
  Testcontainers over an in-memory substitute (H2 standing in for
  Postgres/MySQL) when the project already uses Testcontainers — an
  in-memory DB can silently pass a query that the real production
  database would reject or execute differently.

## Mocking

- Mock/stub only the collaborators the class under test actually depends
  on (Mockito's `@Mock`/`@InjectMocks`, or MockK for Kotlin) — do not
  reach for `@MockitoBean`/`@SpringBootTest` just to avoid constructing a
  plain dependency by hand when the class was designed for constructor
  injection. `@MockitoBean` is the current annotation for mocking a bean
  inside a Spring test slice; Spring Boot's own `@MockBean` is deprecated
  since Boot 3.4 and removed in 4.x, so only reach for it on a project
  still pinned below 3.4.
- Assert on behavior/interactions relevant to the test (`verify(...)`),
  not on incidental implementation details that would make the test
  brittle to a harmless refactor.

## Assertions and determinism

- Use the project's already-configured assertion library (AssertJ,
  JUnit's own assertions, or Kotlin's `kotlin.test`) — do not introduce a
  second one into a project that has standardized.
- Never synchronize with `Thread.sleep`/`delay` to wait for an async
  result — use Awaitility (`await().atMost(...).until(...)`), a
  `CompletableFuture`/coroutine join, or a `CountDownLatch`, so the test
  is deterministic and not flaky under CI load.
- A test covering validation should assert on the specific constraint
  violation/exception type, not just that "some" exception was thrown.

## Fixtures and lifecycle

- Prefer a builder or a test-fixture factory method for constructing
  entities/DTOs with sensible defaults, overriding only the fields the
  test cares about, over duplicating a large constructor call in every
  test method.
- Use `@BeforeEach`/`@AfterEach` (or Kotlin's equivalents) for
  per-test setup/teardown; reserve `@BeforeAll`/`@AfterAll` for
  genuinely expensive, safely-shared setup (a Testcontainers instance).

## Coverage expectations

- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix gets a
  regression test that fails before the fix and passes after.
- Run the project's build-tool test task (`./gradlew test` or `mvn test`)
  for any change touching test files, and its integration-test task
  separately when one is configured, before reporting done.
